        <div id="third-party-tested" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Is BSN Syntha-6 Third-Party Tested?
          </h2>

          <p>
            We found no sport certification for Syntha-6: no NSF Certified for Sport, Informed Sport or Informed Choice claim on the Amazon titles or the retailer label pages we checked at the time of writing. The retailer listings say only that it is made in the USA in a GMP (Good Manufacturing Practices) facility. GMP is a manufacturing standard, not independent testing of the finished product for banned substances or contaminants.
          </p>

          <p>
            That contrasts with its sister brand. BSN and Optimum Nutrition are both owned by Glanbia, and Optimum Nutrition promotes Informed Choice testing on many Gold Standard flavors, with Banned Substance Tested in its Amazon titles. Syntha-6 listings carry no equivalent wording. If BSN adds a certification later, it should appear on the tub and in the certifier's public database, which is the only place we would treat it as confirmed.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">No badge on the tub, no assumption.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">If you are tested under WADA, NCAA or a pro league, choose a powder listed in the NSF Certified for Sport or Informed Sport database and check the lot number. Syntha-6 is not a product we could confirm in either at the time of writing.</p>
          </div>

          <p>
            For everyone else, the lack of a certification is not proof of a problem; it simply means you are relying on the brand's own quality control. Milk-based powders generally fare better than some plant proteins in published heavy-metal surveys, but no public heavy-metal results for Syntha-6 were available to us, so we cannot say how it compares. Our <a href="/is-protein-powder-safe/">protein powder safety guide</a> explains what the different seals do and do not cover.
          </p>

          <p>
            If testing is the deciding factor, you have options without giving up on a whey and casein style. Several powders in our <a href="/best-casein-protein-powder/">best casein protein powder</a> list carry a named certification, and Gold Standard is the closest like-for-like swap from the same parent company.
          </p>
        </div>

