        <div id="taste" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. What Do Buyers Say About Syntha-6 Taste and Mixing?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Taste is the main reason people buy Syntha-6, and buyers consistently describe it as thick, creamy and milkshake-like, especially in milk. We have not tasted it ourselves; this summary comes from the patterns in retailer and Amazon buyer reviews at the time of writing. It regularly appears in conversations about the <a href="/best-tasting-protein-powder/">best-tasting protein powders</a> for exactly that reason.
          </p>

          <p>
            The texture has a clear source on the label. The sunflower creamer, MCT powder and casein fractions all thicken a shake, and casein in particular gives a heavier, more dairy-like body than whey alone. That same thickness makes Syntha-6 popular for mixing into oats, yogurt and puddings; it is one of the picks in our <a href="/best-protein-powder-for-oatmeal/">protein powder for oatmeal</a> guide.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Which flavors do buyers rate highest?</h3>

          <p>
            Chocolate Milkshake and Vanilla Ice Cream are the flavors buyers mention most and the ones with the most retailer listings, which usually signals the best sellers. Cookies and Cream, Chocolate Peanut Butter and Strawberry Milkshake also draw steady praise, and Mint Chocolate Chip and Chocolate Cake Batter are offered in some sizes. Flavor preference is personal, so if you are new to the brand, the smaller tub is a reasonable test even though it costs more per gram.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What do buyers complain about?</h3>

          <p>
            The recurring complaints in buyer reviews are the low protein per scoop for the size of the tub, price increases over the years, and reports that a flavor tasted different after a formula change. Some buyers also say it can clump if added to water before shaking, so liquid first and a shaker bottle help. A smaller group notes that the extra fiber and sweeteners do not sit well with them, which is worth keeping in mind if you have a sensitive stomach.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="vs-gold-standard" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. BSN Syntha-6 vs Optimum Nutrition Gold Standard: Which Is Better?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Gold Standard is the better buy on protein and price, and Syntha-6 is the better buy on texture: per the labels, Gold Standard gives 24 g of protein in 120 calories for about $1.49 per 25 g, while Syntha-6 Original gives 22 g in 190 calories for about $1.80 per 25 g at the Amazon prices we saw. Both are artificially sweetened and both come from Glanbia brands.
          </p>

          <p>
            Pick Gold Standard if you mix with water, track calories, want the most protein per dollar or care about the Informed Choice testing that Optimum Nutrition promotes. Pick Syntha-6 if you drink your shake as a treat, like a thick casein-style texture, or use it as a filling snack between meals where the extra calories and fiber help. Our full <a href="/optimum-nutrition-protein-powder-review/">Optimum Nutrition protein powder review</a> covers the ON range in more detail.
          </p>

          <p>
            Syntha-6 also competes with other multi-protein blends. Today's sibling review of <a href="/1st-phorm-protein-powder-review/">1st Phorm protein powder</a> looks at another premium-priced, flavor-led brand, and the <a href="/pro-jym-protein-powder-review/">Pro JYM review</a> covers a blend of whey, casein and egg that is built more for protein per scoop. If you want to put Syntha-6 head to head with any powder in our database, the <a href="/protein-powder-comparison/">comparison tool</a> lines up label figures and cost per 25 g side by side.
          </p>

          <p>
            Against Dymatize, the gap is similar. Dymatize Elite and ISO100 are both denser in protein than Syntha-6 Original and promote Informed Choice testing, as our <a href="/dymatize-protein-powder-review/">Dymatize protein powder review</a> explains. Syntha-6 wins neither on numbers nor on testing; it wins only on the creamy, dessert-like style that its fans keep coming back for.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="who-should-buy" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. Who Should Buy Syntha-6, and Who Should Skip It?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Syntha-6 suits people who want a protein shake that tastes like a dessert and do not mind about 216 calories per 25 g of protein to get it. It is a reasonable choice for casual lifters, people who struggle to finish plain whey shakes, and anyone using a shake as a filling snack or a slow-digesting option before bed.
          </p>

          <p>
            It is also a reasonable fit for people trying to gain weight, since the extra fat and carbohydrate add calories without much effort, although a dedicated mass gainer or extra food will do more; see our <a href="/best-protein-powder-for-weight-gain/">protein powder for weight gain</a> guide. For people on a cut, Syntha-6 Isolate or Edge is the better choice in the range, or a lean whey isolate from our <a href="/best-whey-protein-isolate/">best whey protein isolate</a> list.
          </p>

          <p>
            Skip Syntha-6 if you are a drug-tested athlete, since we found no sport certification. Skip it if you are lactose intolerant: all three versions contain whey concentrate or milk protein, and even the Isolate includes milk protein isolate. Skip it if you avoid sucralose and acesulfame potassium, or if you need dairy-free or soy-free protein, since the label states milk and soy.
          </p>

          <p>
            As with any protein powder, people with kidney disease, those who are pregnant or breastfeeding, anyone with a milk allergy, and older adults managing medical conditions or medications should check with a doctor or registered dietitian before adding it. And remember that a powder only fills a gap; our <a href="/how-much-protein-do-i-need/">how much protein do I need</a> guide helps you work out whether you need a daily shake at all.
          </p>
        </div>

