            11. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Comparing tubs instead of protein</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A 5 lb tub of Syntha-6 Original holds about 1,056 g of protein, while a 5 lb tub of Gold Standard holds about 1,776 g. Judging by tub size or price per tub hides that gap. Always divide the price by the number of 25 g protein portions before deciding which tub is the bargain.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying the Original while cutting calories</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The Original adds about 216 calories for every 25 g of protein, roughly 90 more than a lean whey. Two shakes a day can quietly add 180 calories. If you are in a calorie deficit, Syntha-6 Isolate or Edge gives you the same style with far fewer calories per gram of protein.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Assuming Syntha-6 Isolate is pure whey isolate</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Syntha-6 Isolate blends whey protein isolate with milk protein isolate, which still contains casein and some lactose. It also uses a creamer with sodium caseinate. People who choose isolate for lactose reasons should read the panel and consider a pure whey isolate or a lactose-free powder instead.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Trusting old reviews about egg protein</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Older Syntha-6 labels listed egg albumen, and that detail still circulates in reviews. The current US label we checked lists milk proteins only and states milk and soy as allergens. Formulas change, so read the panel on the tub you buy rather than relying on an old write-up.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Treating GMP as third-party testing</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The retailer listings mention GMP manufacturing, which governs how a factory runs, not independent testing of each batch. Drug-tested athletes need a product listed in the NSF Certified for Sport or Informed Sport database. We could not find Syntha-6 in either at the time of writing.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
